  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Clustered toughshank (Collybiopsis confluens) is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its clustered growth pattern and tough, fibrous stem. This species belongs to the Omphalotaceae family and is commonly found throughout New England forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Clustered Growth",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies grow in dense clusters or tufts from a common base, making this the most reliable field identification feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tough Stem",
      "description": "The stalk is notably tough and fibrous, difficult to break cleanly, distinguishing it from more fragile species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Size",
      "description": "Small caps (1-3 cm) with a grayish-brown to tan coloration and a slightly depressed or convex shape",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Clustered toughshank grows on decaying wood, leaf litter, and mossy substrates in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The specific epithet 'confluens' refers to the mushrooms' characteristic clustering behavior, as multiple fruiting bodies appear to merge or flow together from a single mycelial source.",
